  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled conditions return today into Saturday with \nscattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms developing.\n\nEarly this morning, a weak cold front is situated off to our west \nwhile high pressure remains anchored well offshore. Showers and a \nfew thunderstorms are currently located just west of the forecast \narea, with a few additional showers/storms located to our southeast. \nOutside of a low chance for a stray shower or storm across far NW or \nSE portions of the area, mainly dry conditions are anticipated \nthrough sunrise.\n\nThe front slowly moves into the area later this morning before \neventually stalling across eastern portions of the area. Surface \nheating this afternoon through broken cloud cover will boost MLCAPE \nvalues to ~1500 J/kg. Scattered to widespread showers and storms are \nexpected to develop between ~2 and 5 PM EDT along sea/bay breeze \nboundaries and along the stalled front. There is just enough mid-\nlevel flow (25 to 30 knots) combined with steep low-level lapse \nrates, that a few storms may become strong or severe with damaging \nwind gusts being the primary threat. SPC has all of the area \nhighlighted in a Marginal Risk (1 out of 5) for severe weather \ntoday. \n\nIn addition to a severe weather threat today, torrential downpours \nare also expected with PWATs climbing to 2.00\"+. WPC has the \nsoutheastern portion of the area (including much of Hampton Roads) \nhighlighted in a Day 1 Slight ERO (2 out of 5) with the remainder of \nthe area in a Marginal ERO (1 out of 5). The 00z HREF highlights the \nSE portion of the area with a 30% contour of >3\" in the 3 hours QPF, \nwhich usually results in an elevated flooding threat. Isolated to \nscattered instance of flash flooding will be possible later today, \nespecially across urban or poor drainage areas. Rain chances linger \ninto the first part of the night before generally dry conditions \nreturn after midnight into early Saturday morning. \n\nThe front weakens but will continue to linger across far SE VA into \nNE NC on Saturday. Scattered showers and storms will continue across \nour southern areas Saturday afternoon, with the highest coverage \nclosest to the Albemarle Sound. WPC has locations south of the VA/NC \nborder highlighted in a Day 2 Marginal ERO for Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of next week.\n\nAn expansive 500mb ridge (1.5 to 2 standard deviations above normal) \nbuilds from the Central Plains into the Mid Atlantic and Southeast \nearly next week. Both NBM and deterministic guidance continue to \nfavor strong diabatic heating Monday through Thursday with highs in \nthe low to mid 90s for inland areas. 850mb temperatures of 19-21C \nand strong 1000-850mb thickness schemes support deep mixing during \npeak heating, which should pull afternoon dew points down into the \nupper 60s to around 70. Peak heat indices will likely stay below \n105, generally hovering around 100-104 each day. Rain chances remain \nminimal through Wednesday with increasing rain chances later in the \nweek as another front approaches.",
